//! Repository hygiene: no tracked text file may carry a reference to an AI
//! assistant or tool vendor (names, "generated by", co-author trailers), or to
//! the client context the public snapshot was redacted of (ADR 0097: the first
//! consumer's name and code identifiers, the withheld usage survey's section
//! labels, the work domain). The project's docs, comments and fixtures must
//! read as ordinary engineering prose that names no client.
//!
//! Scope is `git ls-files` minus `vendor/` (upstream code we do not own),
//! lockfiles (tool-written headers), `LICENSE` (canonical GPL text) and
//! anything that is not valid UTF-8 (PDFs, fonts, images, binaries). Genuine
//! false positives are allow-listed one `(path, line substring)` pair at a
//! time in `ALLOW`; exclusions are deliberately not widened to directories so
//! a new violation next to a legitimate one still fails. This file spells the
//! assistant terms out, so it excludes itself by exact path; the client terms
//! appear here only as digests (`HASHED`).

use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
use std::process::Command;

/// AI assistants, tool vendors and development tool names, matched
/// case-insensitively as plain substrings. These are not secret, so they are
/// spelled out.
const TERMS: &[&str] = &[
    "claude",
    "anthropic",
    "chatgpt",
    "openai",
    "copilot",
    "ai assistant",
    "generated by",
    "co-authored-by",
    // Names of the development tooling and workflows used on this project
    // (ADR 0097); the public docs describe the work, not the tools.
    "codebase-memory",
    "codebase-docs",
    "bug-grind",
    "bug grind",
    "graphify",
];

/// A blocked term known only by its digest, so that the public tree does not
/// spell out the client context it was redacted of (ADR 0097): the first
/// consumer's name, its code identifiers and their distinctive fragments, the
/// withheld usage survey's section label, and the work domain and mailbox.
///
/// A window of `len` bytes of a lowercased line matches when its rolling hash
/// agrees with `check` (a 12-bit prefilter, too short to recover the term
/// from) and its SHA-256 equals `sha256`. The digests are dictionary-checkable
/// by someone who already guesses a term; the point is that a reader of the
/// public tree cannot read one off. `phrase` terms must end at a word boundary,
/// as in `matched_term`. To add a term, run
/// `WORDLIST_TERM='...' cargo test -p pdf-extract-testkit --test wordlist -- --ignored --nocapture print_hashed_entry`
/// and paste the line it prints; never commit the term itself.
struct Hashed {
    len: usize,
    check: u64,
    phrase: bool,
    sha256: &'static str,
}

